{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region= Manawatū-Whanganui}}
'''Palmerston North''' is a parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The electorate was first formed for the {{NZ election link|1890}} and was called '''Palmerston''' until 1938. The current MP for Palmerston North is [[Tangi Utikere]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. He has held this position since the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]].

==Profile==
In December 1887, the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] voted to reduce its membership from general electorates from 91 to 70. The 1890 electoral redistribution used the same 1886 census data used for the 1887 electoral redistribution. In addition, three-member electorates were introduced in the four main centres. This resulted in a major restructuring of electorates, and Palmerston was one of four electorates to be first created for the 1890 election.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=54ff}}

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Palmerston North.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Palmerston North reached its current approximate size at the expense of the old [[Manawatu (New Zealand electorate)|Manawatu]] electorate in the lead up to the introduction of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]]. The boundaries of the Palmerston North electorate were last adjusted in the 2007 redistribution, when the electorate became fully urban and covered all of the urbanised part of [[Palmerston North]] City, with the towns in its orbit such as [[Ashhurst]] and [[Linton, New Zealand|Linton]] becoming part of the neighbouring electorate of [[Rangitīkei (New Zealand electorate)|Rangitīkei]]. To counter the population loss on the Manawatu River's left bank, the section of the city on the right bank, including the suburb of [[Milson, New Zealand|Milson]], was moved in at the same time.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9 |date=14 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123193826/https://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |archive-date=23 January 2019 |url-status=dead }}</ref> No boundary adjustments were undertaken in the subsequent 2013/14 redistribution.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9 |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would expand to include the outer suburbs of [[Aokautere]], [[Fitzherbert]], [[Longburn]], [[Turitea]], [[Whakarongo]] and the town of [[Bunnythorpe]].<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=3 September 2025 |page=8 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

At the [[2013 New Zealand census|2013 census]], the Palmerston North electorate reported the highest share of those working in the retail trade industry (11.4%); those whose occupation was a community and personal service worker (10.9%); and those whose households used [[natural gas|mains (natural) gas]] as a heating fuel. Among general electorates, Palmerston North had the second-highest share of those working in the education and training sector (11.8%), and people affiliated with the Brethren religious denomination (1.2%).<ref name="Electorate profile">{{cite web |title= Palmerston North electorate profile|url= https://www.parliament.nz/resource/en-NZ/DBHOH_Lib_EP_Palmerston_North_TOC/4b63b99810a390f40df5d3baea1cfc66a3be90ab |date= December 2017 |publisher=Parliamentary Library |access-date= 8 November 2018}}{{CC-notice|cc=by4|author=the Parliamentary Library}}</ref>

==History==
The electorate has been loyal to the Labour Party, not having elected a National MP since [[1975 New Zealand general election|1975]], and having not re-elected a National MP since [[1966 New Zealand general election|1966]].

The first representative of the Palmerston electorate was [[James Wilson (New Zealand politician, born 1849)|James Wilson]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=148}} Wilson previously represented the {{NZ electorate link|Foxton}} electorate until its abolition in 1890.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=157}} In the {{NZ election link|1890}}, Wilson beat [[Frederick Pirani]] by 61 votes.<ref name="DNZB Pirani">{{cite web|url= http://www.dnzb.govt.nz/dnzb/default.asp?Find_Quick.asp?PersonEssay=2P19|title=Pirani, Frederick 1858–1926 |last=Oliver|first=Steven |date=22 June 2007 |publisher=Dictionary of New Zealand Biography|access-date=13 December 2011}}</ref> In the {{NZ election link|1893}}, Wilson stood successfully in the [[Ōtaki (New Zealand electorate)|Otaki electorate]]{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=148}} and Palmerston was won by Pirani, who was confirmed in {{NZ election link year|1896}} and {{NZ election link year|1899}},{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=132}} but defeated in {{NZ election link year|1902}} when he contested the {{NZ electorate link|Hutt}} electorate.<ref name="1902 Hutt election">{{cite news |title=Untitled|url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=EP19021129.2.56.5 |access-date=12 December 2011|work=[[The Evening Post (New Zealand)|The Evening Post]] |date=29 November 1902 |volume=LXIV |issue=131 |page=6}}</ref> In the 1899 election, Prime Minister [[Richard Seddon]] expressed his opposition to Pirani (who had previously stood for the Liberal Party) by endorsing [[William Thomas Wood]], who came second that year.<ref name="DNZB Pirani" />

Wood was the successful candidate in the 1902 election, and he was confirmed in {{NZ election link year|1905}}.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=149}} The {{NZ election link|1908}} was held under the Second Ballot Act, contested by three candidates. [[David Buick (politician)|David Buick]], Wood and W. Milverton received 2675, 2626 and 123 votes, respectively.  As Buick did not receive an absolute majority, a second ballot was required.<ref name="1908 Palmerston, first">{{cite news|title=Palmerston |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=WH19081118.2.36 |access-date=14 December 2011|work=[[The Wanganui Herald]]|volume=XXXXIII |issue=12621 |date=18 November 1908|page=5}}</ref>  Buick standing for the [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform Party]] was again successful in the second round of voting and was thus elected.<ref name="1908 Palmerston, second">{{cite news|title=In other Districts |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=NEM19081125.2.11 |access-date=14 December 2011|work=[[Nelson Evening Mail]]|date=25 November 1908|volume=XLII|page=2}}</ref>  Buick was re-elected in {{NZ election link year|1911}} and {{NZ election link year|1914}}, but died in office on 18 November 1918 during the [[1918 flu pandemic|influenza epidemic]].<ref name="David Buick parliamentary bio">{{cite web|title=David Buick : Member of Parliament for Palmerston North 1908–1918 |url= http://www.parliament.nz/NR/rdonlyres/0FFC0F10-5B94-497D-84A3-10CECB6D93FF/135381/49SCLGE_EVI_00DBHOH_BILL8602_1_A32138_PalmerstonNo.pdf |publisher=New Zealand Parliament|access-date=19 December 2011}}</ref>

[[Jimmy Nash]], the sitting [[Mayor of Palmerston North]], won the resulting [[1918 Palmerston by-election|by-election]] on 19 December 1918.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=128}} Nash was confirmed at the next five general elections,{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=128}} but was defeated in the {{NZ election link|1935}} by [[Joe Hodgens]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] in an election also contested by the then-mayor, [[Gus Mansford]].<ref name="DNZB Hodgens">{{DNZB|Atkinson|Neill|4h31|Hodgens, Joseph |23 December 2011}}</ref> Hodgens had previously contested the electorate in the {{NZ election link year|1922}} and {{NZ election link year|1931}} elections. He retired from the electorate at the 1946 election due to the deteriorating health of his wife.<ref name="DNZB Hodgens" />

The {{NZ election link|1946}} was contested between [[Ormond Wilson]] for Labour and Mansford, whose relationship with the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] was strained, since his 1935 election campaign had contributed to the defeat of Nash. Mansford failed to get nominated by the National Party for the {{NZ election link year|1938}} and {{NZ election link year|1943}} elections.  So in 1946, the National Party decided not to stand a candidate, with Mansford running as an [[Independent politician|independent]]. Wilson obtained a majority of 928 votes.<ref name="Ormond Wilson parliamentary bio">{{cite web |last=Hancock |first=Mervyn |title=George Hamish Ormond Wilson : Member of Parliament for Rangitikei 1935–1938 Palmerston North 1946–1949 |url=http://citylibrary.pncc.govt.nz/user/file/280/(9)%20George%20Hamish%20Ormond%20Wilson.pdf |publisher=Palmerston North Library |access-date=26 December 2011 |date=December 2005 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120325085814/http://citylibrary.pncc.govt.nz/user/file/280/(9)%20George%20Hamish%20Ormond%20Wilson.pdf |archive-date=2012-03-25 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Wilson lost the subsequent election in {{NZ election link year|1949}} against [[Blair Tennent]] of the National Party.<ref name="DNZB Ormond Wilson">{{DNZB|Paul|Janet|5w37|Wilson, George Hamish Ormond - Biography|23 December 2011}}</ref><ref name="Tennent DNBZ">{{DNZB|Pilkington |Dorothy |5t13 |Tennent, William Blair - Biography|21 October 2011}}</ref> Tennent was confirmed at the subsequent election, but then lost against the Labour candidate, [[Philip Skoglund]], in the {{NZ election link|1954}}.<ref name="Tennent DNBZ" /> Skoglund contested the {{NZ election link|1957}} against [[Bill Brown (New Zealand politician)|Bill Brown]] of the National Party and was confirmed by the voters. However, in the {{NZ election link|1960}}, Brown beat Skoglund by the narrow majority of 123 votes. At the {{NZ election link|1963}}, Brown had a 772-vote majority to Skoglund. The {{NZ election link|1966}} was contested by Brown against Labour's [[Joe Walding]], with Brown holding a 259-vote majority. Brown died in office on 16 October 1967 at a function in [[Kaiapoi]].<ref name="Brown parliamentary bio">{{cite web |last=Hancock |first=Mervyn |title=George Hamish Ormond Wilson : Member of Parliament for Palmerston North 1960–67 |url=http://citylibrary.pncc.govt.nz/user/file/283/(12)%20William%20Brown.pdf |publisher=Palmerston North Library |access-date=28 December 2011 |date=December 2005 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120407025214/http://citylibrary.pncc.govt.nz/user/file/283/%2812%29%20William%20Brown.pdf |archive-date=7 April 2012 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

The resulting {{By-election link|Palmerston North|1967}} was contested by five candidates: Walding (Labour Party), Gordon Cruden (National Party), [[John O'Brien (New Zealand politician)|John O'Brien]] ([[Social Credit Party (New Zealand)|Social Credit Party]]), Goldingham (Progress) and P. J. Wedderspoon ([[Democratic Labour Party (New Zealand)|Democratic Labour]]). Walding obtained a majority of 592 votes.<ref name="Walding parliamentary bio">{{cite web |last=Hancock |first=Mervyn |title=Joseph Albert Walding : Member of Parliament for Palmerston North 1967–1975 1978–1981 |url=http://citylibrary.pncc.govt.nz/user/file/284/(13)%20Joseph%20Albert%20Walding.pdf |publisher=Palmerston North Library |access-date=25 December 2011 |date=December 2005 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120407025404/http://citylibrary.pncc.govt.nz/user/file/284/(13)%20Joseph%20Albert%20Walding.pdf |archive-date=7 April 2012 }}</ref> Walding represented the Palmerston North electorate until the {{NZ election link|1975}}, when he was defeated by [[John Lithgow (New Zealand politician)|John Lithgow]], and from {{NZ election link year|1978}} to 1981. Six months prior to the {{NZ election link|1981}}, he announced his retirement from Parliament.<ref name="Walding parliamentary bio" />

The Labour nomination was hotly contested and [[Trevor de Cleene]], a good friend of Walding, was the most experienced candidate who put his name forward for selection, and despite concerns about his often controversial nature, he was nominated by the party. The candidate put forward by National was his old foe [[Brian Elwood]], with whom he had worked on the Palmerston North City Council for many years, and against whom he lost the mayoralty contest in 1974. Elwood and de Cleene received 8315 and 10425 votes, respectively (representing 35.7% and 48.5% of the vote), with de Cleene thus entering [[New Zealand Parliament|Parliament]] in 1981.<ref name="De Cleene parliamentary bio">{{cite web |last=Hancock |first=Mervyn |title=Trevor Albert De Cleene : Member of Parliament for Palmerston North 1981–1990 |url=http://citylibrary.pncc.govt.nz/user/file/286/(15)%20Trevor%20Albert%20de%20Cleene.pdf |publisher=Palmerston North Library |access-date=28 December 2011 |date=December 2005 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120325090208/http://citylibrary.pncc.govt.nz/user/file/286/(15)%20Trevor%20Albert%20de%20Cleene.pdf |archive-date=2012-03-25 |url-status=dead }}</ref>  De Cleene won the {{NZ election link|1984}}, called early by [[Robert Muldoon]], with an increased majority over National's candidate, C G Singleton.<ref name="De Cleene parliamentary bio" /> In the {{NZ election link|1987}}, de Cleene raised his share of the vote to 56.2%, defeating National's Paul Curry. He did not seek re-election in the {{NZ election link|1990}}.<ref name="De Cleene parliamentary bio" />

[[Iain Lees-Galloway]] was selected by the Labour Party as successor to retiring MP [[Steve Maharey]], who became Vice Chancellor of [[Massey University]], in a contested Labour Party selection for the {{NZ election link|2008}}.<ref name="man about the house">{{cite news |last = Dykes | first = Mervyn|url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/manawatu-standard/features/2372797/Man-about-the-House |title=Man about the House |work= Manawatu Standard |access-date=12 December 2011 |date=29 April 2009}}</ref> Lees-Galloway defeated the National Party candidate, Malcolm Plimmer, with a majority of 1,117 votes.<ref name="results 2008">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results -- Palmerston North |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2008/electorate-38.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Officer |access-date=11 December 2011}}</ref><ref name="Lees-Galloway parliamentary profile">{{cite web |title=Iain Lees-Galloway |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/current/51MP2751/lees-galloway-iain |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=5 October 2014}}</ref> In the {{NZ election link|2011}}, Lees-Galloway was confirmed with an increased majority of 3,285 votes, defeating National's Leonie Hapeta.<ref name="results 2011">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results -- Palmerston North (2011) |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2011/electorate-38.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Officer |access-date=11 December 2011}}</ref> In the {{NZ election link|2014}}, Lees-Galloway was challenged by the [[Mayor of Palmerston North]], [[Jono Naylor]], but remained successful.<ref>{{cite news |title=Lees-Galloway returned as MP |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/manawatu-standard/news/10524338/Lees-Galloway-returned-as-MP |access-date=4 October 2014 |work=[[Manawatu Standard]] |date=20 September 2014}}</ref> Lees-Galloway had a decreased majority of 2,212 votes over Naylor.<ref name='2014election'/> Naylor was the lowest-ranked National MP who entered Parliament via their list.<ref>{{cite news| url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/manawatu-standard/10580275/Naylor-confirmed-as-MP |first=Lucy |last=Townend |title=Naylor confirmed as MP |work=[[Manawatu Standard]] |date=4 October 2014 |access-date=4 October 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Rutherford |first1=Hamish |title=National loses majority, Greens pick up one |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/election-results-2014/10580208/National-loses-majority-Greens-pick-up-one |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006125906/http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/election-results-2014/10580208/National-loses-majority-Greens-pick-up-one |url-status=dead |archive-date=6 October 2014|access-date=4 October 2014 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=4 October 2014 }}</ref> Ian Lees-Galloway retired before the 2020 election and was replaced by [[Tangi Utikere]], the city's deputy mayor, as the electorate's MP.

